00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.090
No, I don't, but I-

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:47.100
I happen to have bought some good soil.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.290
That's my- I honestly
have like a black thumb.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:51.930
Everything takes way longer
to grow than you think.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:53.460
I'm super grateful for grocery stores.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:54.480
We would all be dead

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.842
if we were living off of my
sustainable farming skills.

00:00:56.842 --> 00:00:57.960
(laughing)

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.210
So let me say that first and foremost.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:01.080
We'd all dead.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.850
We'd have no food,

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:03.813
maybe a strawberry.

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:09.600
But the experience that
it has given us has been

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.010
like we thought we were
gonna kind of just do it

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:12.210
a little bit over COVID,

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:13.860
and then it just kind of stuck.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:14.693
I'll tell you a story.

00:01:14.693 --> 00:01:15.526
I was like, "you know what?"

00:01:15.526 --> 00:01:16.620
We were in Canada,

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.810
and I became friends with
this woman named Judith

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:20.580
who was in the house next door to us.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.200
And she was lovely towards our kids.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.360
She was like a grandma
and she took our kids on

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:25.890
and would take 'em to the library.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:27.600
Like she was this beautiful older woman.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.310
And so she farmed on her rooftop,

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:31.680
and she would grow these
unbelievable tomatoes.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:32.880
I mean tomatoes that I have-

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.800
I was like, "this is what a
tomato's supposed taste like."

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.080
She goes, "Okay, you have to go to,"

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:40.110
It was something like
heirloomtomatoes.com,

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:41.250
and I was like, "Judith."

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:42.630
She goes, "No, I swear."

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:43.463
So I do.

00:01:43.463 --> 00:01:45.000
I go on heirloomtomatoes.com,

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.500
and I buy every strand I
can to grow in a region.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:50.640
They all took.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:51.690
I didn't do anything.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:52.770
I planted them.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:54.930
I put some soil, I watered,

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:56.490
I learned that you have
to water the ground,

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:57.420
not the actual plant.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:59.250
You learn a little bit of things,

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.290
but now I'm only in one business.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.150
So I'm only in the tomato business.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:04.410
Like I have no other-

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.750
I'm not the smartest at figuring it out.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.090
So like at one point I
grew hundreds of carrots.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:10.080
Hundreds.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.020
No one needs hundreds
of carrots all at once.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:13.380
Right. Right.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:16.920
So I feel like my timing on
my farming is questionable.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:19.500
But we did grow tomatoes,
carrots, zucchinis,

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.050
watermelon I'm still growing 'cause I, I-

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:24.180
I don't know why but it's
taken like nine months,

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.100
corn, which we harvested,

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.410
and then we grew soybean beans for-

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:31.560
to like nourish the ground,
not to actually eat.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:32.850
Which soybeans grow like weeds.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.680
Wait, so it sounds like
you're quite good at this.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.780
No, I'm really not though. I swear, like

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.870
if something goes wrong I either
Google or call my gardener,

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.250
like I don't know enough.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:42.163
Well, you're on your way.

00:02:42.163 --> 00:02:44.910
I mean we started over COVID,

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:46.500
we started composting,

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.330
and my wife got really-

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:49.890
Liza got really into it,

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:52.320
and what I didn't realize
is it's all about the worms.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:53.153
Yeah.

00:02:53.153 --> 00:02:56.760
In your- and so she kept going
out to check on the worms

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:58.020
and make sure the worms were okay,

00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.550
and it got to the point where it's like,

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:03.780
okay it's, you know, it's
time to watch our HBO Max show

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:04.613
or our Netflix show,

00:03:04.613 --> 00:03:06.927
and she'd be like, "I gotta
go check on the worms."

00:03:06.927 --> 00:03:07.760
(chuckling)

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.731
And then I realized that I
was fighting for attention-

00:03:09.731 --> 00:03:10.564
Yeah.

00:03:10.564 --> 00:03:11.456
From my wife, with worms.

00:03:11.456 --> 00:03:12.289
I knew that's where this was headed.

00:03:12.289 --> 00:03:13.590
And it was- yeah, but it was-

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:15.570
it enraged me 'cause there was-

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:17.647
there's been like 18 years of,

00:03:17.647 --> 00:03:21.330
"Okay we have these kids and
I understand that I'm second

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:23.614
to these children and
that they come first."

00:03:23.614 --> 00:03:24.617
You got replaced by worms.

00:03:24.617 --> 00:03:26.430
I got replaced by worms.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.170
The timing is interesting.

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:29.003
Yeah.

00:03:29.003 --> 00:03:30.600
I know, I know exactly.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.150
She saw that they're like
getting ready to leave the nest

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:36.510
and she said "Anything, anything,

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:38.220
it doesn't even have to be sentient.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.260
It doesn't have to have consciousness

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:41.940
or a soul."

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:43.380
Just worms.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:46.170
We all have this fantasy of
not needing to go anywhere,

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:48.120
like I don't need to
go to the supermarket.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:49.050
Yeah.

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:50.496
We still do.

00:03:50.496 --> 00:03:51.329
I'm not- this is- you know.

00:03:51.329 --> 00:03:53.910
And also all the things
I like can't be grown.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:54.743
Right.

00:03:54.743 --> 00:03:55.576
Yeah, exactly.

00:03:55.576 --> 00:03:57.540
But we also- are we try-
when we built our house,

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:00.660
we try to make it as off the
grid as the city would let us.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:02.703
So we are, well water,

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:06.420
can't drink it but we do
everything else with well water,

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.737
and then we're fully 100% solar-powered.

00:04:08.737 --> 00:04:09.570
Oh wow.

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:11.280
Why can't you drink the well water?

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:12.297
It's just not drinkable quality.

00:04:12.297 --> 00:04:13.560
Yeah. Okay.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:14.790
The city doesn't recommend it,

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:16.560
but you can like use it to-

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:17.393
you can do all- like you can use-

00:04:17.393 --> 00:04:18.720
You can irrigate with it.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:19.980
You can do everything
you need to do with it.

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:20.970
So that- yeah, exactly.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:22.620
So we try to be super sustainable.

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:24.540
Did you buy a property specifically

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:25.560
'cause you knew it had a well?

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:26.393
No.

00:04:26.393 --> 00:04:27.226
It was just there?

00:04:27.226 --> 00:04:28.059
You randomly dig.

00:04:28.059 --> 00:04:30.197
I swear, you spend 5- it's $5,000,

00:04:30.197 --> 00:04:33.630
$5,000 and they go, "yeah,
well $5,000 a shot."

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:35.910
And they honestly come with some tools

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:39.709
something and they go, "Mm, there."

00:04:39.709 --> 00:04:40.542
Oh no.

00:04:40.542 --> 00:04:44.130
No, no. I- I've had experience
with this years ago where

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:46.920
I lived in a place that you
could dig a well and you-

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:49.200
someone comes out, and they dig the well,

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.720
and you pay them to dig the well,

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:56.310
you know, with with- with a
giant drill that drills down.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:58.417
But they dig the well, they're
there all day and they go,

00:04:58.417 --> 00:04:59.880
"Yep. Nothing there."

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:00.713
Yeah.

00:05:00.713 --> 00:05:02.410
"So you owe me 5,000."

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:03.243
(laughing)

00:05:03.243 --> 00:05:04.110
Literally, you like-

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:05.647
it's weird, but you're just like,

00:05:05.647 --> 00:05:08.430
"Oh maybe he'll find the,
the aquifer. I don't know."

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:10.620
We got lucky and the guy happened to have-

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:11.940
we wait, we didn't know.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:13.440
We could have struck out and had nothing,

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:14.940
but we're like, "let's just try and see."

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:15.773
Oh.

00:05:15.773 --> 00:05:18.750
And so we dug a hole and they're
like, "Yeah, you got it."

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:20.520
And we were like, "Whoa. It happened."

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:22.140
But it's a- it's a crapshoot.

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:23.800
Do you have critters? Do you have like-

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:24.633
Tons.

00:05:24.633 --> 00:05:25.466
What kind of critters do you have?

00:05:25.466 --> 00:05:28.170
Not including like rats,
mice, like the- like that?

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:29.423
I include rats and mice as critters.

00:05:29.423 --> 00:05:30.840
They're all God's creatures.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:32.550
They were on the arc with Noah.

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:33.456
I wish they weren't.

00:05:33.456 --> 00:05:35.706
(laughing)

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:37.680
Noah let everyone on.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:38.513
He was like, "What?

00:05:38.513 --> 00:05:40.107
No, we need a male and female COVID."

00:05:41.413 --> 00:05:42.246
Yeah.

00:05:42.246 --> 00:05:43.079
"Noah, you don't need to bring in-"

00:05:43.079 --> 00:05:44.547
"Nope. We gotta bring 'em all in."

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:47.190
Yeah. Some could have been exed out.

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:48.023
What do we have?

00:05:48.023 --> 00:05:49.620
All right, I'll tell you
about the serious sightings.

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:50.580
We have rattlesnakes.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:51.413
We have-

00:05:51.413 --> 00:05:52.903
Oh okay. That I'm not doing.

00:05:52.903 --> 00:05:54.755
I- I can't do rattlesnakes.

00:05:54.755 --> 00:05:55.703
I- I can't either.

00:05:55.703 --> 00:05:58.650
It's not like I- I don't disagree.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:05:59.483
It's awful.

00:05:59.483 --> 00:06:00.316
It's terrifying.

00:06:00.316 --> 00:06:01.149
Yep.

00:06:01.149 --> 00:06:03.600
I- I'm gonna name drop here, but I had a-

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:05.970
A fancy friend that you text back.

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:07.530
Text back immediately.

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:10.680
Pee-wee Herman invited me over to his-

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:11.580
we were gonna go out to dinner.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:12.930
He said, "Yeah, just stop by my house."

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:16.920
And so I walk up and his house
is like, it's all kind of

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:20.670
he's got lots of plants and
everything everywhere and it's-

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:23.820
it feel- it's- it's very kind of natural.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:26.700
And so you're making your
way through this narrow path,

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:29.850
and it's lots of, you know,
shrubs that have grown out,

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:30.930
and you get to the door,

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:31.763
and I finally got to the door,

00:06:31.763 --> 00:06:33.600
and I knocked on the door, and
he let me in, and we chatted

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:34.680
for a little bit and
then he was like, "Okay

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:35.580
it's time to go."

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:39.990
And he stepped outside, and
he put on giant special boots,

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:40.950
and I said, "What are those for?"

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:43.530
And he said, "Oh, rattlesnakes
all over the place.

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:46.140
So when I walked to the car,
I wear these," and I said-

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:48.780
and it was the same path
that I had just come up on,

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:51.720
and I'm not, I'm- you
know, wearing, you know

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.750
open-toed sandals and short shorts

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:56.400
'cause that's how I roll.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:57.660
With little dead mice hanging off them.

00:06:57.660 --> 00:06:59.160
Yeah, exactly.

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:00.780
With bacon wrapped around them.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:02.880
And I was like, "Paul you didn't-"

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:05.247
and he was like, "Oh
you're probably okay."

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:07.830
Well that- that seems excessive.

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:09.720
Okay. So it's not like
we have ours, just like,

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:10.740
greeting you at the door,

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:13.110
but we've had like two
on our property this year

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:14.370
and then tons of gopher snakes.

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:15.210
You don't care about those.

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:16.043
They're good.

00:07:16.043 --> 00:07:17.820
They kind of eat all the mice and rats.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:19.560
We did see like a mouse outside.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:20.550
And then we have bunnies.

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:23.340
We have a big bunny
population in our backyard.

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:25.590
Is a bunny just a rabbit,
it's just a nicer term?

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:27.690
Yeah. It's a cute term for rabbit.

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:29.160
Yeah. Bunny. It's a rabbit.

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:31.394
All right. I didn't know if
there was a separate, like

00:07:31.394 --> 00:07:32.790
in children's books, they're a bunny.

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:33.623
Like kitty.

00:07:33.623 --> 00:07:34.560
It's like kitty.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:35.393
But there's a hare.

00:07:35.393 --> 00:07:36.900
And a hare has those big flop ears.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:37.770
We don't have those.

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:40.140
We definitely have like the
cottontail wild bunnies.

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:40.973
Okay.

00:07:40.973 --> 00:07:41.940
Then we have a bobcat.

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:42.773
We have a mountain lion.

00:07:42.773 --> 00:07:45.030
We have a family of deer,

